-3.  Apply the power transformation with exponent $\alpha$: $t' = P(t_{scaled}) = t_{scaled}^{\alpha}$ [S1].
-    *   Note: The source describes this as curving metric distance to have a steep gradient near the origin [S1]. The specific function form implied by "power transformation" with parameters $(10000x, -0.25)$ is $P(x) = x^{-0.25}$ [S1].
-4.  Compute the distortion loss using the transformed distances $t'$ instead of the original metric distances $t$ [S1]. The standard distortion loss formula involves the weights $w_k$ and distances $t_k$:
-    $$ L_{dist} = \sum_k w_k |t_k - \sum_j w_j t_j| $$
-    In this variant, $t_k$ and $t_j$ are replaced by their transformed counterparts $t'_k$ and $t'_j$ [G].
+- Scaling factor $s$: 10000 [S1].
+- Power exponent $\lambda$: -0.25 [S1].
+- Distortion loss multiplier $\alpha$: 0.005 [S1].
 
